  "query_preview": "..\n\nKEY MESSAGE 1...Hot temperatures and elevated humidity again\ntoday will lead to increased potential for heat related \nillnesses.\n\nKEY MESSAGE 1 DESCRIPTION...\nLow pressure and a shortwave trough swing out of the region \nearly this morning, as another shortwave and cold front move in \nfor Saturday. Westerly winds continue to reduce any seabreeze \neffects, so heat will be felt throughout all of the forecast\narea. Today is going to be hot, with ambient temperatures in \nthe upper 80s to low 90s in the north, and mid-90s in the \nCentral Highlands and Downeast regions. Slight improvement is\nexpected in terms of humidity, but still muggy throughout \nnorthern and eastern Maine. Minimum relative humidities \ngenerally in the 30s to 40s are expected. Heat Advisories\ncontinue for Southern Aroostook, Upper Penobscot Valley, and \nInterior and Coastal Downeast. A cold front begins to pass \nSaturday into Saturday evening, helping temperatures begin to \ndrop back into the 80s. \n\nSmoke from Canadian wildfires makes its way down into Maine as\nwell, which could act as cloud cover to keep temperatures in the\nlower-to-mid 90s. Air Quality Alerts are in effect through \nSunday for reduced visibility and degraded air quality at times.\n\nKEY MESSAGE 2...Isolated thunderstorms remain possible this \nevening through Saturday. A conditional threat for severe storms\nexists.\n\nKEY MESSAGE 2 DESCRIPTION...\nA cold front moves through the region Friday evening into \nSaturday which could provide some lift for isolated \nthunderstorms. Low-level lapse rates, CAPE, and DCAPE are \nfavorable, but require strong forcing to go anywhere. Mid-level \nlapse rates and shear are mediocre. Thus, only a slight chance \nfor some isolated showers and thunderstorms is forecast late \nFriday into Saturday.",
